0.024383544921875, -0.0285797119140625, 0.0005979537963867188, 0.00579833984375, 0.022491455078125, 0.0207061767578125, -0.0017147064208984375, -0.0034656524658203125, 0.01123046875, 0.005176544189453125, -0.007144927978515625, 0.0199432373046875, -0.008392333984375, 0.04193115234375, 0.006999969482421875, 0.0311737060546875, -0.004970550537109375, 0.053497314453125, 0.0244598388671875, 0.0017290115356445312, 0.0020961761474609375, -0.01288604736328125, -0.0192413330078125, 0.02752685546875, -0.0137176513671875, -0.0240631103515625, 0.01094818115234375, 0.0176849365234375, 0.006633758544921875, -0.015228271484375, 0.004329681396484375, -0.01024627685546875, -0.005924224853515625, 0.0301513671875, -0.01392364501953125, 0.0135040283203125, -0.007419586181640625, -0.01430511474609375, 0.01617431640625, -0.0265350341796875, -0.0338134765625, 0.024566650390625, 0.0096282958984375, 0.01296234130859375, 0.0097198486328125, 0.026611328125, 0.032684326171875, 0.0008058547973632812, -0.01419830322265625, -0.015716552734375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nPurpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024. A multi-agent AI pipeline was developed and deployed locally to perform report structuring and quality assurance (QA). The pipeline consisted of three primary agents, each with a distinct function and stepwise workflow:\n\n1. Sectioning Agent (assigns sentences to anatomical sections):\n   - **Summary:** The Sectioning Agent segments each report into predefined anatomical sections, using rule-based and LLM-based methods to ensure each sentence is assigned to the most appropriate section.\n   - **Workflow:**\n     1. Each sentence is scanned for explicit section headers and anatomical terms using a predefined list of regular expression (regex) patterns.\n     2. If a sentence matches a single, unambiguous regex pattern, it is assigned to the corresponding anatomical section.\n     3. If a sentence matches multiple patterns, none, or if the context is ambiguous (e.g., overlapping anatomical references or implicit section transitions), the sentence is passed to a local large language model (LLM; Llama-2-13B, default parameters, no fine-tuning).\n     4. The LLM receives as input the ambiguous sentence along with its preceding and following sentences and the list of possible anatomical sections. The LLM outputs the most appropriate anatomical section for the sentence, based on contextual understanding.\n     5. The final section assignment for each sentence prioritizes regex-based assignments when unambiguous, and defers to the LLM only when regex rules are inconclusive or conflicting.\n     6. LLM outputs were audited during development: a sample of 100 LLM-assigned sentences was manually reviewed for accuracy, and any systematic errors led to refinement of prompts or fallback to manual assignment.\n     - **Example:** If a sentence mentioned \"pleural effusion\" without a clear section header and regex could not resolve the section, the LLM would use the context of surrounding sentences to assign it to the \"Pleura\" section.\n\n2. Consistency Agent (checks for cross-section consistency of key findings):\n   - **Summary:** The Consistency Agent ensures that key clinical findings are consistently documented across the Findings and Impression sections.\n   - **Workflow:**\n     1. Identifies key findings (e.g., \"pulmonary embolism,\" \"liver lesion\") using a curated list of clinical terms.\n     2. Checks for the presence or absence of these findings in both Findings and Impression sections.\n     3. Flags discrepancies if a key finding appears in one section but not the other, or if terminology or anatomical references are inconsistent (e.g., \"right renal mass\" in Findings but \"left renal mass\" in Impression).\n     4. Discrepancies are defined as any mismatch in laterality, organ, or critical finding between sections.\n     5. Rules were iteratively refined using a development set of 50 reports, with manual review to adjust term lists and logic for improved sensitivity and specificity.\n\n3. QA Agent (flags gender-anatomy conflicts and critical communication documentation):\n   - **Summary:** The QA Agent detects gender-anatomy conflicts and checks for documentation of communication regarding critical findings.\n   - **Workflow:**\n     1. Identifies gender-anatomy conflicts (e.g., references to male anatomy in female patients) using keyword matching (e.g., searching for \"prostate\" in female patients).\n     2. Uses LLM-based contextual analysis to flag potential issues not captured by keywords. The LLM receives the full report text and patient demographic data, and outputs a list of potential gender-anatomy conflicts or other QA issues.\n     3. For critical findings, checks for the presence of communication documentation (e.g., \"results communicated to referring physician\") when a critical finding is detected.\n     4. LLM outputs for QA were reviewed in a development set of 50 reports to ensure accuracy, with errors leading to prompt refinement or additional keyword rules.\n\nThe agents operated sequentially: the Sectioning Agent output fed into the Consistency Agent, followed by the QA Agent. Each report passed through all agents, and flagged issues were aggregated for review.\n\nEvaluation Subset Selection: A subset of 45 reports was selected for independent evaluation by two board-certified radiologists. Stratified random sampling was used to ensure proportional representation of all flagged error types (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, and undocumented critical findings) and unflagged reports. The full dataset was divided into strata based on the presence and type of QA flags, and reports were randomly sampled from each stratum in proportion to their prevalence in the full dataset. This approach ensured that each flagged error type and unflagged reports were represented in the evaluation subset according to their frequency, while also guaranteeing inclusion of rare error types. The subset size of 45 was chosen to balance feasibility of detailed manual review with the need to include sufficient examples of each error type. Specifically, with 90 flagged reports in the full dataset (14.1%), a sample of 45 allowed for inclusion of at least 5\u201310 examples of each error type, including rare categories, while keeping the review workload manageable. However, this sample size limits statistical power and the precision of confidence intervals, and may not fully capture the variability present in the full dataset. As such, results from the evaluation subset should be interpreted as preliminary and not fully generalizable.\n\nEvaluation Metrics: \n- **Operational Definitions:** \n  - \"Correctly restructured\" was defined as the report being segmented into anatomical sections such that each sentence was assigned to the appropriate section without altering the original clinical content or meaning. Specifically, each sentence about a particular anatomical structure (e.g., \"hepatic steatosis\") should appear only in the corresponding section (e.g., \"Liver\"), and no sentences should be omitted, duplicated, or moved to an incorrect section. \n  - \"Incorrectly restructured\" indicated that the restructuring introduced errors in section assignment (e.g., a lung finding placed in the \"Liver\" section), omitted sentences, or altered the intended meaning (e.g., splitting a sentence so that its clinical context was lost). Edge cases included sentences describing findings relevant to multiple organs (e.g., \"There is a mass at the hepatic flexure abutting the liver and colon\"), which were considered correct if placed in either relevant section, but incorrect if omitted or placed in an unrelated section.\n  - **Reviewer Rubric:** Reviewers were provided with a rubric specifying that: (a) all sentences must be present and assigned to the correct anatomical section; (b) no sentences should be assigned to multiple sections unless clinically justified (e.g., findings spanning two organs); (c) the clinical meaning of each sentence must be preserved; and (d) any deviation from these criteria constituted an incorrect restructuring. Example: If the sentence \"There is a right lower lobe nodule\" appeared in the \"Liver\" section, this was scored as incorrect. If the sentence \"Mild hepatic steatosis\" was correctly placed in the \"Liver\" section, this was scored as correct. If a sentence described a finding at the interface of two organs, assignment to either section was acceptable.\n- QA performance ratings were assigned on a 3-point scale: \"excellent\" (all relevant issues correctly flagged, no errors or omissions), \"good\" (minor errors or omissions not affecting clinical interpretation, such as a missed typographical inconsistency), and \"fair\" (errors or omissions with potential to affect clinical interpretation, such as missing a critical finding or misclassifying a major discrepancy). Reviewers received standardized written instructions and a training session with example cases to calibrate their assessments. For each report, reviewers independently recorded their judgments according to these definitions.\n\nReviewer Disagreement Handling: In cases where reviewers disagreed on restructuring or QA ratings, disagreements were resolved by consensus discussion. If consensus could not be reached, a third board-certified radiologist adjudicated the case. For the primary outcome (proportion of correctly restructured reports), consensus ratings were used in all analyses.\n\nStatistical Analysis: Reviewer agreement was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic. For key proportions (e.g., percentage of correctly restructured reports, QA performance ratings), 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated using the Wilson score interval. For QA flagging, precision, recall, and F1-score were calculated for each error type (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, undocumented critical findings) using reviewer consensus as the reference standard. The primary outcome was the proportion of reports correctly restructured by the system, as determined by reviewer consensus. Secondary outcomes included the accuracy of QA flagging (true/false positives and false negatives for each error type), precision, recall, F1-score, and inter-rater agreement statistics. Effect sizes were not calculated because the study was descriptive and not designed to compare interventions or groups; as such, the implications are that the results characterize system performance but do not quantify the magnitude of improvement over baseline or alternative methods. Comparative analyses (e.g., before/after restructuring) were not performed, as the study focused on system validation rather than intervention efficacy.\n\nResults:\n\nSystem Performance\n\n| Metric                                      | Full Dataset (n=638) | Evaluation Subset (n=45) |\n|---------------------------------------------|----------------------|--------------------------|\n| Total sentences structured                  | 22,270               | 1,570                    |\n| Reports flagged for \u22651 QA issue             | 90 (14.1%)           | 15 (33.3%)               |\n| - Section mismatches                        | 80 (12.5%)           | 13 (28.9%)               |\n| - Gender-anatomy conflicts                  | 7 (1.1%)             | 2 (4.4%)                 |\n| - Undocumented critical"}}